Definition 2.8

We say that D is an elementary region in the plane if it can be described as a subset of R² of one of the following three types: 

	Type 1 (See Figure 5.22)

		D = {(x, y) | γ(x) =< y =< δ(x), a =< x =< b}, where γ and δ are continuous on [a, b]. 

	Type 2 (See Figure 5.23): 

		D = {(x, y) | α(y) =< x =< β(y), c =< y =< d}, where α and β are continuous on [c, d]. 

	Type 3 D is of both type 1 and type 2
